   "summary": "Both defects reproduced and fixed, uncommitted.\n\n1. tag.rs `observed_local_tag`: only `rev-parse --verify --quiet` exit 1 with empty stdout is Absent. Any other non-zero exit, a missing exit code, or exit 1 with output now returns `GraphError::ReleaseCommand` with `CommandFailure::NonZeroExit` carrying stderr, as the for-each-ref call beside it does.\n\n2. registry.rs npm observation is now `classify_npm_view`, with `npm_error_code`.\n- Exit 0 with output is Exact, as before.\n- A failure whose stdout parses as JSON with `error.code` exactly `\"E404\"` is Absent. The match is case- and length-exact, so `e404` and `E4040` are not Absent.\n- Every other failure is a transient Indeterminate `CommandFailed`, retried by the bounded policy. That covers other JSON codes (E401, E403, E429, E5xx, ETARGET, ECONNREFUSED, EAI_AGAIN, ETIMEDOUT), non-JSON stdout, empty stdout, and JSON of the wrong shape.\n- Free text is never read. The old `etarget`, `no matching version` and `is not in this registry` substring matches are removed.\n- Exit 0 with empty stdout is now transient Indeterminate `MalformedResponse`. It was Absent.\n\nWhat npm really does, checked with npm 11.16.0 against the live registry:\n- A missing package (`callisto-no-such-package-zz@1.0.0`) exits 1 and prints JSON `{\"error\":{\"code\":\"E404\",...}}` on stdout.\n- A missing version of a real package (`left-pad@9.9.9`) exits 1 with the same JSON shape, `E404`, summary \"No match found for version 9.9.9\". It does not exit 0 empty and does not use ETARGET.\n- An unreachable registry (`--registry http://127.0.0.1:9/`) exits 1 with JSON code `ECONNREFUSED`.\n- `npm view left-pad@1.3.0 nosuchfield --json` exits 0 with empty stdout. Callisto passes no field, so a real observation never hits that case. An empty success therefore proves nothing and fails closed.\n- An older npm that exits 0 empty for a missing version would now be blocked instead of publishing. I could only verify 11.16.0.\n- A private package read without credentials is answered E404 by the registry. That is indistinguishable from absent and is documented in the code.\n\nEnd-to-end check: a test in release_execution.rs drives the real `execute_release` with the production `TagProvider` and a git runner whose local `rev-parse` exits 128. The run returns a `ReleaseCommand` NonZeroExit error. The effect counter stays 0, and the only git calls are `remote` and `rev-parse` (no `tag`, no `push`). With the tag fix temporarily reverted, the same test failed with `ReleaseInvariant \"the effect must not run\"`, so the effect was reached. The fix is restored.\n\nSibling audit (what turns a non-zero exit, empty output or free text into Absent). Only the two defects above were changed.\n- tag.rs remote `ls-remote`: non-zero exit, timeout or I/O error becomes transient Indeterminate. Exit 0 with no matching line is Absent; git exits 0 when nothing matches (captured fixture `ls-remote-absent.txt`). Intended, unchanged.\n- cargo `classify_cargo_info`: Absent needs exit 101 and the spec-scoped text ``could not find `NAME@VERSION` ``. That is a free-text match, but cargo has no JSON mode and the captured network-failure fixture proves it is not fooled. Yanked reads as Absent by design. Intended, unchanged.\n- github.rs: the REST 404 is Absent, which is the documented answer. Other statuses are Indeterminate. Non-200 GraphQL is Indeterminate. GraphQL 200 with `errors` or a null repository is CommandFailed. A null `release` is Absent. A non-zero `gh` exit with no parseable HTTP response is an error. Intended, unchanged.\n- forge.rs: Absent only from the github.rs lookup, plus a draft release under `PublishedOnly`. Intended, unchanged.\n- artifact.rs: Absent when the release is absent, or when the successfully read `assets` array has no entry of that name. Intended, unchanged. One unverified concern: if `GET /releases/{id}` truncates `assets` at a page limit for a release with many assets, an existing asset could read as Absent. I did not verify that against GitHub and did not change it.\n- PyPI (`classify_pypi_simple`, per the brief): a curl failure with no parseable response is CommandFailed. A parsed 404 is Absent, and Absent when no file matches the version. Unchanged, per the brief.\n- Tag edge not changed: a local `refs/tags/<name>` that points to a non-commit object makes `^{commit}` peeling exit 1 silently, which now reads as Absent. This is the same as before, and `git tag` creation would then fail loudly. I could not check it with real git because git commands are refused in this sandbox. A `for-each-ref` confirmation on exit 1 would close it.",
